00:07:04.189 --> 00:07:08.670
OK, so let's start with genomics 101.

00:07:08.670 --> 00:07:11.350
The first part is, what are variants?

00:07:11.350 --> 00:07:14.670
Because you're going to be hearing a lot of most

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:17.509
of our students are conflicted variants.

00:07:17.509 --> 00:07:23.589
So a variant is essentially a variation of a person's DNA

00:07:23.589 --> 00:07:26.550
from, let's say, a person's gene.

00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:29.470
There are two sort of categories

00:07:29.470 --> 00:07:31.149
that we break them into.

00:07:31.149 --> 00:07:34.629
One is germline, which is essentially inherited.

00:07:34.629 --> 00:07:36.310
You can inherit from your parents,

00:07:36.310 --> 00:07:41.350
or there can be something that is, when a person is

00:07:41.350 --> 00:07:43.709
conceptualized, let's say, when they are born,

00:07:43.709 --> 00:07:47.029
it is essentially a de novo variation.

00:07:47.029 --> 00:07:48.149
Or it can be acquired.

00:07:48.149 --> 00:07:51.230
So it can be a variation that your DNA has acquired

00:07:51.230 --> 00:07:53.269
over your legacy.

00:07:53.269 --> 00:07:55.589
There are multiple different risk factors

00:07:55.589 --> 00:08:00.069
that can actually cause that to happen.

00:08:00.069 --> 00:08:04.269
So the key takeaway from this side of the issue

00:08:04.269 --> 00:08:06.670
is cancer is not a single event.

00:08:06.670 --> 00:08:10.509
It is, let's say, a process that can take years,

00:08:10.509 --> 00:08:11.949
or let's say decades.

00:08:11.949 --> 00:08:15.389
So it starts with DNA damage, mutation,

00:08:15.389 --> 00:08:19.470
then it goes on to, let's say, a particular size

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:22.470
where there is no regulation possible by the tumour

00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:24.829
compressor.

00:08:24.829 --> 00:08:28.389
It reaches a particular mass at which

00:08:28.389 --> 00:08:29.750
the station becomes cancer.

00:08:29.750 --> 00:08:32.070
And if it is allowed to go without treatment,

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:34.269
it can go into a metastasis stage, which

00:08:34.269 --> 00:08:38.879
is sort of a stage.

00:08:38.879 --> 00:08:43.759
Then what does the journey look like for a patient?

00:08:43.759 --> 00:08:45.559
So they said a pretty long journey

00:08:45.559 --> 00:08:47.720
for a particular patient.

00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:51.240
It starts with somebody having some symptoms.

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:54.360
At some point, they decide that this does not seem to go away.

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:58.960
I should do a CTF, see my GP.

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:00.120
They go there.

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:02.000
The GP tries to resolve it.

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:03.480
They observe it for some time.

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:06.080
That is when they would actually send it.

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:08.240
They would refer it for a particular scan,

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:10.759
for some particular test.

00:09:10.759 --> 00:09:13.399
If the scan shows something unusual,

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:16.960
that is when they would involve an oncologist.

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:20.639
The oncologist would essentially look at it, observe it.

00:09:20.639 --> 00:09:23.240
And if they see merit, this is when

00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:27.480
they would recommend essentially doing a biopsy,

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:30.080
after which essentially actually

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:32.960
H plus E scanning is done, or let's say,

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:36.600
a genomic testing is actually recommended.

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:39.360
If this is actually, at that point,

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:41.159
they know whether the tumour is aggressive,

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:45.159
what kind of tumour it is, how to essentially approach it,

00:09:45.159 --> 00:09:46.360
what the treatment can look like,

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:48.960
and this is where essentially the treatment starts.

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:49.799
And of course, after that,

00:09:49.799 --> 00:09:52.279
there is a continuous process of scans,

00:09:52.279 --> 00:09:53.519
monitoring, et cetera.

00:09:53.519 --> 00:09:58.519
So this, the vertical, the horizontal bar below

00:09:58.519 --> 00:10:03.360
essentially is a loop that will sort of continue.

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:06.960
An important takeaway from this is this surgery,

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:09.759
sorry, this genomic testing is essentially

00:10:09.799 --> 00:10:13.120
the part where the actual treatment essentially starts.

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:17.039
And hence, this is a sort of very critical step

00:10:17.039 --> 00:10:18.600
for the patient themselves,

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:19.840
and they are actually looking forward

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:23.279
to feeling better as soon as they can.

00:10:23.279 --> 00:10:26.080
So this was sort of the genomic samples.

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:31.779
Now let's move on to a day in the life of a pathologist.

00:10:31.779 --> 00:10:34.019
So a genomic pathologist.

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:39.179
She, looking at the genomic analysis

00:10:39.179 --> 00:10:41.100
is not the only job that she does.

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:44.580
She had actually a varied set of responsibilities.

00:10:44.580 --> 00:10:47.500
She would be doing administrative work.

00:10:49.019 --> 00:10:50.659
She has lab director duties.

00:10:51.539 --> 00:10:52.899
She would be doing vet lab stuff.

00:10:52.899 --> 00:10:56.179
She would have to do departmental reviews.

00:10:56.179 --> 00:10:58.580
She would have to do hematopathyology

00:10:58.580 --> 00:10:59.899
and molecular surveys.

00:10:59.899 --> 00:11:02.340
So there are actually quite a varied set of things

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:04.019
that she needs to do in her day,

00:11:04.019 --> 00:11:05.779
and it's not just essentially looking

00:11:05.779 --> 00:11:10.700
at the genomic results.

00:11:10.700 --> 00:11:12.419
But as I said earlier,

00:11:12.419 --> 00:11:14.779
this is what her day probably looks like,

00:11:14.779 --> 00:11:17.259
looking at my emails, departmental meetings,

00:11:17.259 --> 00:11:20.980
microscopy work, tissue assessment, technical validation.

00:11:20.980 --> 00:11:22.740
And on top of that,

00:11:22.740 --> 00:11:25.179
she might have a long list of cases

00:11:25.179 --> 00:11:27.100
which are waiting for the treatment, right?

00:11:27.100 --> 00:11:28.980
And essentially, each of these cases

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:31.139
are actual patients who are looking forward

00:11:31.139 --> 00:11:36.070
to getting back from the pathology lab.

00:11:36.070 --> 00:11:37.789
So, and on top of this,

00:11:37.789 --> 00:11:40.230
as you know, the complexity is exploding.

00:11:40.230 --> 00:11:45.230
So, 35% of the FDA approved treatments today

00:11:46.350 --> 00:11:48.350
require some sort of genomic analysis.

00:11:49.990 --> 00:11:51.750
The knowledge which used to double,

00:11:52.789 --> 00:11:55.470
let's say in 50 years, is now doubling every year.

00:11:55.470 --> 00:11:57.750
So the knowledge that a pathologist would have

00:11:57.750 --> 00:12:00.830
would be relevant half as much next year.

00:12:00.830 --> 00:12:03.750
So there is no chance a pathologist can keep up

00:12:03.750 --> 00:12:05.509
with all the publications of the new knowledge

00:12:05.509 --> 00:12:07.350
that is coming out.

00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:09.789
Plus, the data from each genomic analysis

00:12:09.789 --> 00:12:11.590
